    "title": "The Imperfect Tense",
    "subtitle": "Habits, descriptions and scenes in the past: all endings, the three irregular verbs and the time words",
    "footer": "Spanish Learning Program · A2 · Verbs · Imperfect",
    "next": "Next: A2 · PDF 4 — Preterite vs imperfect"
  },
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "tip",
      "title": "After this PDF you can",
      "bullets": [
        "describe how things used to be and what you used to do",
        "conjugate every regular verb in the imperfect, plus the only three irregular verbs: ser, ir and ver",
        "describe people, places, weather, age and time in the past",
        "recognise the time words that point to the imperfect"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "h1",
      "text": "1. What the imperfect does"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "The imperfect (<i>pretérito imperfecto</i>) describes the past as a <b>scene in progress</b>: how things were, what used to happen, what was going on. It does not say when something started or ended. Think of a film shot that keeps running."
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "style": "plain",
      "widths": [
        4.6,
        7.4,
        5.0
      ],
      "head": [
        "Use",
        "Example",
        "English"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Habits in the past",
          "De niño jugaba al fútbol cada día.",
          "As a child I used to play football every day."
        ],
        [
          "Description",
          "La casa era grande y tenía un jardín.",
          "The house was big and had a garden."
        ],
        [
          "Age",
          "Tenía diez años.",
          "I was ten years old."
        ],
        [
          "Time",
          "Eran las tres de la tarde.",
          "It was three in the afternoon."
        ],
        [
          "Weather",
          "Hacía frío y llovía.",
          "It was cold and raining."
        ],
        [
          "Feelings and states",
          "Estaba cansado y quería dormir.",
          "I was tired and wanted to sleep."
        ],
        [
          "Action in progress",
          "Veía la tele cuando llegó mi hermano.",
          "I was watching TV when my brother arrived."
        ],
        [
          "Polite request",
          "Quería pedir una información.",
          "I wanted to ask for some information."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "dutch",
      "title": "Dutch ik werkte covers both",
      "bullets": [
        "Dutch <i>ik werkte</i> can mean a repeated past (<i>vroeger werkte ik elke dag</i>) or a single past. Spanish splits them: <b>trabajaba</b> for the habit, <b>trabajé</b> for the single finished event (PDF 4).",
        "Test: if the English can be <b>used to</b>, <b>would</b> or <b>was ...ing</b> (Dutch: <i>vroeger</i>, <i>altijd</i>, <i>was aan het</i>), use the imperfect."
      ]
    },
    {
